  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 \n\nThe extra-tropical low from the remnants of Isaias continues\nto bring rain showers across the Southern Tier into NEPA. These\nshowers will lift north as the afternoon progresses, with rain\nexpected across the area by late afternoon. Moderate rain has\nmoved into the Wyoming Valley region and should slowly spread to\nthe NNE. Along and east of I-81 from the Wyoming Valley into \nOtsego county has the best chances to see 1.0-1.5 inches of rain\nfrom this system. Weak mid-level frontogenesis is progged to \ndevelop over this area and keep periods of moderate rain going \ninto the early overnight hours. The low will kick east of the\narea during the overnight hours, with some lingering lake\nenhanced rain showers possible into the early morning hours. \nSome areas could see some minor urban flooding this afternoon\nand evening, especially across poor drainage areas. Flash\nflooding is not expected given the low rainfall rates and dry\nantecedent conditions in place across the region. Temps today\nshould top out in the 50s, while only falling in to the upper\n40s to low 50s tonight.\n\nRidging builds into the area from the west on Monday, bringing\nWSW winds and dry, warmer weather to the region. Highs on \nMonday are expected to climb into the mid to upper 60s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nThe next weather system looks to impact the area on Tuesday. A\nlow pressure system is forecast to track eastward across the\nGreat Lakes into Ontario as the day progresses. This system\nshould bring rain showers to the area with a chance for\nthunderstorms, a few which could be strong to severe west of\nI-81. We are expected to be in the warm sector of this low, \nwith highs in the 70s and dewpoints in the 60s. This would allow\nfor some marginal instability, 500-1000 j/kg of CAPE, to \ndevelop Tuesday afternoon while 30-40kts of 0-6km bulk shear \nmoves into the area. The uncertainty with thunderstorms\ndeveloping remains high as the models continue to have\ndifferences in timing and location of the low. Also, clouds\nare expected during the morning hours that would limit\ninstability, especially if they hang on longer than currently\nforecast. If we can get some clearing for a few hours, the\natmosphere should have the necessary ingredients to get some\nisolated thunderstorms to develop.\n\nBehind this system, high pressure is expected to control the\nweather pattern into Friday. Our next big  looks to be a very \nstrong trough diving into the area from Central Canada. This \ntrough has been modeled somewhat consistently between most of \nthe major global models so confidence is moderate that we will \nget precipitation on Friday into Saturday. The big question is \nwill this trough be able to tap into cold Canadian air and bring\nthe first taste of winter to the region this weekend. Current \nmodel runs show a blast of cold air getting pulled into the \nregion with this trough, dropping high temps from the upper 50s \nto low 60s Friday to the low to mid 40s Saturday. Friday night \nlows in the low to mid 30s are progged to crash to the mid to \nupper 20s Saturday night. With this cold air, the first \nsnowflakes of the season will be possible on Saturday. \nAccumulations are not expected but cold, elevated surfaces could\nsee a light, patchy dusting by the late afternoon hours.",
